        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "If Stock Y grew purely by simple interest, its annual rate would be 29.17%."
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "The end-of-year 1 value for Stock X was 2.5 times its starting value."
        },
        {
          "letter": "E",
          "text": "Stock Y grew at an exact 25% compound annual growth rate."
        }
      ],
      "correct_answer": "E",
      "explanation": "Let initial value = 100. \nYr1 X = 100 + 150% = 250 (which is 2.5 times - D is True). \nYr2 X = 250 * 0.60 = 150 (which is 1.5 times - A is True). \nYr3 X = 150 * 1.25 = 187.5 (overall 87.5% growth - B is True). \nFor simple interest Y: 87.5 / 3 = 29.166% per year (C is basically True). \nFor compound interest Y: rate r = (1.875)^(1/3) - 1. 1.25^3 = 1.953125, not 1.875! Thus, an exact 25% compound rate is False. E is the false statement.",
      "chart_data": {
